::GEO_U06_L06_GP01::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L06_GP01
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.4
Question:
A figure is enlarged by scale factor 3. How does its area change?
{
=A. It becomes 9 times as large#Area changes by k?, so 3?=9.
~B. It becomes 3 times as large#That is the length effect, not the area effect.
~C. It becomes 27 times as large#That is the volume effect.
~D. It becomes 6 times as large#Doubling the scale factor is not the area rule.
}

::GEO_U06_L06_GP02::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L06_GP02
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.4
Question:
A solid is enlarged by scale factor 2. How does its volume change?
{
~A. It becomes 4 times as large#That is the area or surface area effect.
=B. It becomes 8 times as large#Volume changes by k?, so 2?=8.
~C. It becomes 2 times as large#That is the length effect.
~D. It becomes 6 times as large#This does not use k?.
}

::GEO_U06_L06_GP03::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L06_GP03
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.4
Question:
If the linear scale factor is 5, what is the surface area scale factor?
{
~A. 5#That is the linear effect.
~B. 10#This doubles the scale factor instead of squaring it.
=C. 25#Surface area changes by k?, so 5?=25.
~D. 125#That is the volume effect.
}

::GEO_U06_L06_GP04::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L06_GP04
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.4
Question:
A model car has length scale factor 1/4. What is the volume scale factor?
{
~A. 1/4#That is the linear scale factor.
~B. 1/8#This does not cube the scale factor.
~C. 1/16#That is the area scale factor.
=D. 1/64#Volume changes by k?, so (1/4)?=1/64.
}

::GEO_U06_L06_GP05::
Question ID: GEO_U06_L06_GP05
MLA Standard: MLA.GEO.SIM.4
Question:
Why does volume use k? instead of k?
{
=A. Volume has three dimensions, so the scale factor applies to length, width, and height#Cubing matches three-dimensional measurement.
~B. Volume is always larger than area#The rule is based on dimension, not always on size.
~C. Volume uses square units#Volume uses cubic units.
~D. The exponent is chosen by the shape name#The exponent depends on measurement dimension.
}

